---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import { postBySlug } from '../../lib/blog';
import type { FaqItem } from '../../lib/schema';

const post = postBySlug('how-to-read-a-speedometer');

const KM_PER_MI = 1.609344;
const kmhRows = [30, 50, 60, 80, 100, 110, 120, 130].map((k) => [`${k} km/h`, `${(k / KM_PER_MI).toFixed(0)} mph`]);
const mphRows = [20, 25, 30, 45, 55, 65, 70, 75].map((m) => [`${m} mph`, `${(m * KM_PER_MI).toFixed(0)} km/h`]);

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    q: 'What does a speedometer measure?',
    a: 'Your instantaneous speed — how fast the vehicle is moving at this moment — in miles per hour, kilometres per hour or both. A car speedometer works it out from how fast the wheels or transmission are turning.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the odometer the same as the speedometer?',
    a: 'No. The speedometer shows how fast you are going now; the odometer shows how far the vehicle has travelled in total. They usually share the same speed signal and sit in the same instrument cluster.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I read km/h on my speedometer?',
    a: 'On a US or UK car, km/h is usually the smaller inner ring of numbers; read it where the needle crosses that ring. On cars sold in metric countries, km/h is the main scale. To convert, multiply km/h by 0.62 for mph.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a car speedometer measure velocity?',
    a: 'No — it measures speed. Velocity includes direction, and a speedometer shows the same number whether you are heading north or south. Physics textbooks describe it as reading instantaneous speed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between a speedometer and a tachometer?',
    a: 'The speedometer shows road speed; the tachometer shows engine speed in revolutions per minute, usually in thousands. The red zone on the tachometer marks the maximum safe engine speed.',
  },
];
---

<BlogArticle post={post} faqs={faqs}>
  <p>
    <strong>A speedometer shows your current speed: read the number the needle points to, using the scale for the unit you want.</strong>
    On cars sold in the US and UK the large outer numbers are miles per hour and the smaller inner numbers are kilometres per hour. Between
    the numbers, each small tick mark is an equal step — that’s all there is to it, but the details trip up new drivers and travellers alike.
  </p>

  <h2 id="analog">How to read an analog speedometer</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Find the two labelled numbers</strong> the needle sits between — for example 40 and 60.</li>
    <li><strong>Count the spaces between them.</strong> If there are four spaces between 40 and 60, each tick is 5 mph.</li>
    <li><strong>Count ticks from the lower number.</strong> Two ticks past 40 is 50 mph.</li>
    <li><strong>Read straight on.</strong> Looking at the needle from an angle — from the passenger seat, say — makes it appear to point at a slightly different number.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Most dials label every 10 or 20 mph with minor ticks every 2, 2.5 or 5. Many highlight common speed limits so you can find them at a
    glance, and the needle usually sits near the top of the dial at typical highway speeds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metric">Reading mph, km/h and metric speedometers</h2>
  <p>
    Dual-scale speedometers print both units. In the US and UK, mph is the main scale and km/h the secondary one; in Canada, Europe and most
    of the world it is the other way round. A <strong>metric speedometer reading</strong> is simply in kilometres per hour, and if you are
    driving abroad in a rental car the quickest mental conversion is <strong>km/h × 0.6 ≈ mph</strong> (more exactly × 0.62), or
    <strong>mph × 1.6 ≈ km/h</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <DataTable caption="Common km/h speeds in mph" head={['Metric reading', 'In mph']} rows={kmhRows} />
    <DataTable caption="Common mph speeds in km/h" head={['Reading in mph', 'In km/h']} rows={mphRows} />
  </div>

  <h2 id="digital">Digital speedometers</h2>
  <p>
    A digital speedometer shows your speed as a number, sometimes alongside a graphic dial. Many let you switch between mph and km/h in the
    settings menu, and some project the number onto the windshield as a head-up display. Your phone can do the same: the free
    <a href="/speedometer/">online speedometer</a> is a digital GPS speedometer with a large readout, an analog-style gauge and a windshield
    HUD mode.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-measures">What does a speedometer measure — speed or velocity?</h2>
  <p>
    A speedometer measures <strong>speed</strong>, not velocity. Speed is how fast you are going; velocity is speed in a particular direction.
    Your speedometer shows 50 mph whether you are driving north or south, so it can’t be measuring velocity. In physics terms, the
    speedometer of an automobile reads <strong>instantaneous speed</strong> — your speed at this moment rather than your average over a trip.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How a speedometer works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Mechanical (eddy-current) speedometers</strong>, used for most of the 20th century, are turned by a cable from the transmission.
      A spinning magnet drags an aluminium cup against a hairspring, and the faster it spins the further the needle moves.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Electronic speedometers</strong> count pulses from a vehicle speed sensor or the ABS wheel sensors, and a computer drives the needle
      with a small motor or shows the number on a screen.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GPS speedometers</strong> calculate speed from satellite signals, so they measure your movement over the ground directly and aren’t
      affected by tire size.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because car speedometers count wheel turns, a change of tire size changes the reading — see the
    <a href="/speedometer-calculator/">speedometer calculator</a> — and a fault anywhere in that chain can stop it altogether, as covered in
    <a href="/blog/speedometer-not-working/">speedometer not working</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="odometer-vs-speedometer">Odometer vs speedometer</h2>
  <p>
    They are easy to confuse because they sit together, but they measure different things. The <strong>speedometer</strong> shows how fast you
    are going now. The <strong>odometer</strong> shows the total distance the vehicle has ever travelled, and one or two resettable
    <strong>trip meters</strong> show the distance since you last zeroed them. Both are driven by the same speed signal, which is why a
    failed speed sensor usually stops both.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tachometer">Speedometer and tachometer</h2>
  <p>
    The other big dial on many dashboards is the <strong>tachometer</strong>. It shows engine speed in revolutions per minute, usually marked
    in thousands (“3” means 3,000 rpm). The red section is the <strong>redline</strong>: the maximum engine speed the manufacturer allows.
    Speedometers don’t have a redline — the red zone people mean is on the tachometer. Read together, the two tell you which gear suits the
    road: at a steady 60 mph in top gear most cars are turning roughly 1,500–2,500 rpm.
  </p>

  <h2 id="symbols">Speedometer symbols and lights</h2>
  <p>
    On many cars a small speedometer-and-arrow icon in the cluster is the cruise control indicator, lit (often green) when a cruise speed is
    set; a similar symbol with a different colour or a line may mean a speed limiter is active. Your owner’s manual lists every symbol for
    your model. If the speedometer’s own backlight is dim, check the dashboard brightness control before suspecting a fault.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-high">Why speedometers read high — and go so high</h2>
  <p>
    Speedometers are designed to read slightly above your true speed, never below it; the <a href="/car-speed-test/">car speed test</a> shows
    by how much on your car. And if you have wondered why a family car’s dial runs to 140 or 160 mph, read
    <a href="/blog/why-speedometers-go-so-high/">why speedometers go so high</a>.
  </p>
</BlogArticle>
